<section>
<h2 id="others">Where the others are the right choice<a class="hlink" href="#others">#</a></h2>

<p><strong>yazi, ranger, nnn, or mc</strong>, if you want to stay in one
terminal window and your files are on one machine at a time. Same keys, same
shape. pālana's difference is a second pane on a second host, the route
between them, and ZFS.</p>

<p><strong>Transmit</strong>, if your remotes are buckets. S3, Backblaze,
WebDAV, cloud drives—Panic has spent decades on that breadth and polish, and
pālana will never chase it.</p>

<p><strong>ForkLift</strong>, if you need protocol breadth on a budget—SMB
shares, FTP servers, cloud mounts in one dual-pane window.</p>

<p><strong>Marta</strong>, if your files are all local. Free, fast,
keyboard-driven. No remote side.</p>

<p><strong>muCommander or Double Commander</strong>, if you need open source
<em>and</em> cross-platform. Neither is a native Mac app.</p>

<p><strong>Your terminal</strong>, always. pālana never asks you to leave it:
every plan is a command you can paste, and <kbd>⌘`</kbd> is a real shell on
the focused host. If you already move data between servers by hand, pālana is
those same commands with two panes, a route it works out, and a count before
the delete.</p>

